\section{State Definition}
\label{sec:state}

The estimator maintains a nominal quaternion separately from the additive
navigation and bias states.  With the quaternion written as $q\in\Sph^3$, the
nominal state is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:nominal-state-vector}
\vct{x}_{\mathrm{nom}} =
\begin{bmatrix}
q &
\vct{b}_g^{\top} &
\vct{v}^{\top} &
\vct{p}^{\top} &
\vct{S}^{\top} &
\vct{a}_w^{\top} &
\vct{b}_a^{\top}
\end{bmatrix}^{\!\top}.
\end{equation}
The quaternion is propagated and corrected on $\Sph^3$; it is not updated by
ordinary vector addition.

The covariance is defined for the local error state
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:state-vector}
\delta\vct{x} =
\begin{bmatrix}
\delta\vct{\theta}^{\top} &
\delta\vct{b}_g^{\top} &
\delta\vct{v}^{\top} &
\delta\vct{p}^{\top} &
\delta\vct{S}^{\top} &
\delta\vct{a}_w^{\top} &
\delta\vct{b}_a^{\top}
\end{bmatrix}^{\!\top},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\delta\vct{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^3$ is the small
        left-multiplicative attitude error used by the MEKF
        \cite{Shuster1993_AttitudeRepresentations,MarkleyCrassidis2014_Attitude};
  \item $\vct{b}_g\in\mathbb{R}^3$ is gyroscope bias, modeled as a random walk;
  \item $\vct{v}\in\mathbb{R}^3$ is velocity in the world NED frame;
  \item $\vct{p}\in\mathbb{R}^3$ is position or displacement in the world frame;
  \item $\vct{S}\in\mathbb{R}^3$ is the integral of displacement,
        $\vct{S}(t)=\int_0^t\vct{p}(s)\,ds$, used by the drift-control
        pseudo-measurement;
  \item $\vct{a}_w\in\mathbb{R}^3$ is latent world-frame acceleration with an
        OU process prior;
  \item $\vct{b}_a\in\mathbb{R}^3$ is the \emph{residual} accelerometer bias
        about the deterministic temperature-calibration curve.  It is modeled
        as a slow first-order Gauss--Markov/OU process rather than a random walk.
        The physical bias entering the accelerometer model is
        $\vct b_a^{\mathrm{tot}}(T)=\vct k_a(T-T_{\mathrm{ref}})+\vct b_a$.
\end{itemize}

With both bias states enabled, the additive error-state dimension is
\[
N_X = 3+3+3+3+3+3+3=21.
\]
The stored covariance is
$\mat{P}=\E[\delta\vct{x}\,\delta\vct{x}^{\top}]$.

The residual accelerometer-bias correlation time is denoted $\tau_b$.  The
implementation default is $\tau_b=\SI{3000}{s}$.  The existing
\texttt{set\_Q\_bacc\_rw} API name is retained for source compatibility, but
its argument is interpreted as the continuous driving-noise density of this
OU state.  With the historical density
$(5\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}/\sqrt{s}})^2$ and
$\tau_b=\SI{3000}{s}$, the implied stationary standard deviation is
approximately $\SI{0.01936}{m\,s^{-2}}$.  Thus the short-time process-noise
calibration is unchanged while the formerly neutral bias mode acquires a finite
restoring time scale.

Two distinct acceleration covariances are used throughout the paper.  The
matrix $\matg{\Sigma}_{aw}^{\mathrm{stat}}$ is a \emph{process-model
parameter}: it is the stationary covariance of the OU prior and determines the
discrete process covariance $\matg{Q}_d$.  In contrast,
$\mat{P}_{a_wa_w}$ is the current posterior uncertainty of the estimated
acceleration state.  Changing $\matg{\Sigma}_{aw}^{\mathrm{stat}}$ does not, by
itself, overwrite $\mat{P}_{a_wa_w}$.  Hard initialization may reset the
posterior block and its cross-covariances, whereas the default online
maintenance can only add the positive-semidefinite covariance needed to reach
the current stationary floor; the optional legacy mode restores the historical
raw marginal replacement.
